DAY 13 - PART B: LAB
Same app in dev and prod
Time: 75-90 minutes
==================================================================

STEP 1: Explore built-in namespaces
-----------------------------------
k get ns
k get all -n kube-system
k get lease -n kube-node-lease
k api-resources --namespaced=false | head -20


STEP 2: Create namespaces declaratively
---------------------------------------
mkdir -p ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week2/webapp/{base,dev,prod}
cd ~/k8s-journey/manifests/week2/webapp

vi dev/namespace.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: dev
  labels:
    env: dev

vi prod/namespace.yaml
apiVersion: v1
kind: Namespace
metadata:
  name: prod
  labels:
    env: prod

k apply -f dev/namespace.yaml -f prod/namespace.yaml
k get ns --show-labels


STEP 3: One base Deployment, two environments
---------------------------------------------
vi base/deployment.yaml

apiVersion: apps/v1
kind: Deployment
metadata:
  name: web
spec:
  replicas: 2
  selector:
    matchLabels:
      app: web
  template:
    metadata:
      labels:
        app: web
    spec:
      containers:
      - name: web
        image: YOURUSER/myapp:v1
        ports:
        - containerPort: 5000

Note: no namespace in the file. Choose it at apply time:
  k apply -f base/deployment.yaml -n dev
  k apply -f base/deployment.yaml -n prod
  k get deploy -A | grep web

Same name, two namespaces, no conflict.


STEP 4: Make prod different
---------------------------
Scale prod only:
  k scale deploy web -n prod --replicas=4
  k get pods -n dev
  k get pods -n prod

(Here we used an imperative command. Notice the problem: base/ no
longer describes prod. In Week 8, Kustomize overlays will fix this
properly. For now copy the file:)
  cp base/deployment.yaml prod/deployment.yaml
  sed -i 's/replicas: 2/replicas: 4/' prod/deployment.yaml
  k apply -f prod/deployment.yaml -n prod
  k diff -f prod/deployment.yaml -n prod      # no diff = in sync


STEP 5: Switch default namespace
--------------------------------
kubens dev
k get pods              # dev pods, no -n needed
kubens prod
k get pods
kubens default

Without kubens:
  k config set-context --current --namespace=dev
  k config view --minify | grep namespace
  k config set-context --current --namespace=default


STEP 6: Cross-namespace access
------------------------------
k expose deploy web -n prod --port=80 --target-port=5000
k run tmp -n dev --rm -it --image=busybox:1.36 -- sh
  wget -qO- web.prod          # works: namespaces don't block traffic
  wget -qO- web               # fails: no "web" Service in dev
  exit


STEP 7: Imperative vs declarative race
--------------------------------------
Time yourself:
  a) Imperative: create a deployment "api" with image nginx:1.27,
     3 replicas, in namespace dev, then expose it on port 80.
       k create deploy api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 -n dev
       k expose deploy api --port=80 -n dev
  b) Declarative: generate both as YAML into dev/api.yaml (use
     --dry-run=client -o yaml, separate with ---), delete the
     imperative ones, and apply the file.
       k delete deploy,svc api -n dev
       k create deploy api --image=nginx:1.27 --replicas=3 -n dev --dry-run=client -o yaml > dev/api.yaml
       echo "---" >> dev/api.yaml
       k create service clusterip api --tcp=80:80 -n dev --dry-run=client -o yaml >> dev/api.yaml
       k apply -f dev/api.yaml

Then run "k apply -f dev/api.yaml" again: "unchanged". That is
idempotency.


STEP 8: Delete a namespace (and everything in it)
-------------------------------------------------
k get all -n dev
k delete ns dev
k get all -n dev           # gone

Recreate it from your files in one command:
  k apply -f dev/namespace.yaml && k apply -f base/deployment.yaml -n dev && k apply -f dev/api.yaml


STEP 9: Clean up and commit
---------------------------
k delete ns dev prod
cd ~/k8s-journey && git add . && git commit -m "day13: namespaces"
